// Append-driven growth of a REFERENCE slice, single-threaded, held live across
// tens of thousands of safepoints. This is #1438's repro, kept permanently.
//
// #1438 was filed s1 claiming a six-line append-only program hung under
// self-hosted `bit` + `BIT_GC=stress` while the seed compiled it correctly, and
// located the fault in self-hosted safepoint/stack-map emission. It was not a
// compiler bug at all: the measurement ran `BIT_GC=stress bit run <dir>`, and
// self-hosted `bit` is itself a Bit program, so the policy applied to THE
// COMPILER — which then collected at every back-edge of its own compilation.
// The six-line program never got a chance to be wrong. An empty `print("hi")`
// program "hung" the same way, which is what settled it.
//
// The shape is worth a permanent case regardless, because nothing else here
// covers it: `refslice` appends but reaches only ~40 collections, and `gcchurn`
// discards its allocations instead of retaining them. Here every element ever
// appended stays reachable through exactly one root — the slice header in
// `main`'s frame — and `append` reallocates that buffer repeatedly, so a stale
// or missing entry for `xs` sweeps live strings and the verification walk below
// reads freed memory.
//
// The second loop is the real oracle. It re-walks all 4000 elements at one
// safepoint per iteration, so each string is re-proven live thousands of times
// more, and it compares CONTENT rather than counting: a swept-and-reused body
// is a wrong answer here, not a crash.
fn main() {
  let xs = []string(0)
  let i = 0
  while (i < 4000) {
    xs = append(xs, "e${i}")
    i = i + 1
  }

  let bytes = 0
  let ok = true
  let j = 0
  while (j < len(xs)) {
    bytes = bytes + len(xs[j])
    if (xs[j] != "e${j}") {
      ok = false
    }
    j = j + 1
  }
  print("${len(xs)} ${bytes} ${ok}\n")
}
